{
    "title": "Standard Instrument Approach Procedures, and Takeoff Minimums and Obstacle Departure Procedures; Miscellaneous Amendments",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 02, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-02/pdf/2025-21764.pdf",
    "summary": "The Federal Aviation Administration issued a routine final rule that updates, adds, or removes instrument flight procedures and related takeoff rules at a number of airports across the country. The goal is to keep procedures aligned with current navigation equipment, new obstacles, and changing traffic needs, improving safety and efficiency\u2014especially in poor weather.\n\nPilots, airlines, air traffic teams, airports, and passengers all stand to benefit. Updated procedures can lower delays, smooth arrivals and departures, and maintain safe clearances from terrain and structures. Several busy and regional airports are included, from Cincinnati/Northern Kentucky International to Evansville, Prineville, and smaller fields in Alabama, North Carolina, and elsewhere. Some changes take effect later in December, with more following in January, and all the official charts and documents are available online at no cost.\n\nThere may be some downsides. Pilots and operators will need to review the new procedures, update their onboard databases and training, and adjust checklists. A few procedures are being canceled, which could reduce options during low ceilings at certain airports and prompt occasional diversions. Because some updates are tied to immediate safety needs, they may become effective in less than 30 days, compressing planning time. Smaller flight schools and charter operators may incur minor costs from training and data updates, though the agency expects the overall economic impact to be minimal.\n\nThis action is part of the agency\u2019s regular cycle to keep the national airspace current. Rather than printing every chart in the Federal Register, the rule points users to the official procedure documents and charts maintained online. For anyone who flies or manages operations, the takeaway is simple: check the latest procedures for your airports before you go, because several have changed.",
